deep.reconstruction:
This page contains no mathematics. Its only heading, written in ink and underlined, is the French interjection 'Eh bien!' ('Well then!'). Below it is a numbered list of eleven three-digit numbers in strictly increasing order: 1. 226, 2. 232, 3. 242, 4. 270, 5. 274, 6. 276, 7. 304, 8. 317, 9. 318, 10. 347, 11. 368. Entries 1–9 are in ink. Entries 10 and 11 are in fainter pencil, so they were probably added later. The rest of the page is blank.

The remaining marks are not Gauss's and come from the archive:
- the pencil shelfmark 'Math. 6' at the top;
- the circled '21';
- the struck-through older signature '40k/19';
- the pencil folio number '22' at the right margin.

Interpretation (mine, not certain): the format is a heading phrase plus an increasing list of numbers in the range of typical book pagination. This strongly suggests an index of page numbers. The most likely reading is the places where the expression 'Eh bien!' occurs in some French book Gauss was reading. Gauss is known to have kept reading notes and to have studied languages (including French) by close reading. An alternative is a list of pages to look up, in a book or periodical that cannot be identified from this sheet.

The gaps between successive entries are 6, 10, 28, 4, 2, 28, 13, 1, 29, 21. They show no arithmetic structure, which supports the reading as page references rather than a computed sequence. There is no connection to the previous page, which lists longitudes and latitudes of German towns with some side arithmetic. The sheet seems to have been filed in Math. 6 only by its physical association.
